-
-In modern deep hedging, this policy is typically represented by a neural network and trained end-to-end. This approach is attractive because it does not require the world to be exactly Black--Scholes, does not assume frictionless continuous rebalancing, and can incorporate practical features such as transaction costs, trading bounds, incomplete hedge sets, and high-dimensional state variables. In this sense, deep hedging generalizes classical delta hedging: instead of prescribing a rule analytically from a stylized model, it learns a rule directly from data and the training objective.
-
