-\subsection{Why Empirical Validation Is the Missing Step}
+\subsection{Why the Empirical Test Matters}
 \label{subsec:empirical_gap}
 
-The original ProtoHedge results are important, but they do not by themselves establish that the method is useful in realistic hedging environments. Synthetic experiments are valuable because they allow careful control over the data-generating process and clean benchmarking against known baselines. However, they do not fully capture the empirical conditions under which hedging systems are actually deployed.
-
-Historical market data introduce several sources of difficulty that do not appear cleanly in stylized simulation. Market dynamics are non-stationary; realized option paths reflect changing volatility regimes, crisis periods, and structural breaks; transaction costs and rebalancing constraints matter; and the available hedge signals are noisy and imperfect. Moreover, the interpretability question becomes more demanding on real data. In a synthetic environment, a prototype may be interpretable because the world itself is low-dimensional and model-generated. In historical data, a prototype is only useful if it corresponds to a recognizable market state and if its usage is stable enough to support economic interpretation.
-
-This creates three empirical questions that the original ProtoHedge paper leaves open.
-
-First, does ProtoHedge remain competitive with vanilla Deep Hedging on held-out historical data? A prototype bottleneck is only worthwhile if it preserves most of the risk-management quality of the black-box benchmark.
-
-Second, does the prototype structure behave sensibly under realistic constraints? A learned hedging policy can appear strong in aggregate while relying excessively on hard position limits or other boundary effects. In the empirical setting, such behavior is economically important, because a model that achieves performance only by living at its constraints is less credible than one that performs similarly in the interior of the feasible region.
-
-Third, does the interpretability claim survive contact with real data? If ProtoHedge is to be more than a proof-of-concept, then prototype usage should be concentrated enough to be meaningful, should vary in an intelligible way across market regimes, and should be relatable to actual historical episodes rather than only abstract feature vectors.
-
-For these reasons, empirical validation is not a minor extension but a necessary step in evaluating ProtoHedge as a hedging framework.
-
-\subsection{What This Paper Tests}
-\label{subsec:what_this_paper_tests}
-
-This paper studies ProtoHedge precisely along that missing empirical axis. We evaluate it on real historical hedging episodes constructed from market data, compare it against classical baselines and vanilla Deep Hedging, and examine whether its prototype-based interpretability mechanism remains meaningful outside synthetic settings.
-
-Our empirical design focuses on two liabilities. The primary task is hedging a short at-the-money European call over fixed-length historical episodes. We then extend the same framework to a path-dependent short at-the-money Asian call, which tests whether the conclusions persist when the liability depends on the realized path of the underlying rather than only its terminal value. In both cases, the goal is not merely to report raw performance, but to assess the performance--interpretability tradeoff under realistic frictions and constraints.
-
-More specifically, the paper tests the following claims.
-
-\begin{enumerate}
-    \item ProtoHedge remains competitive with vanilla Deep Hedging on real historical data across a panel of underlyings, rather than only in synthetic environments.
-    \item ProtoHedge can improve or preserve downside risk characteristics without relying more heavily than vanilla on hard trading or position boundaries.
-    \item The prototype layer remains empirically interpretable: usage is concentrated in a relatively small subset of prototypes, varies systematically across market regimes, and can be connected to recognizable historical states.
-    \item These conclusions are not limited to a terminal European-call liability, but extend to a path-dependent Asian-call setting under the same evaluation framework.
-\end{enumerate}
-
-This framing also clarifies what the paper does \emph{not} attempt to show. Our objective is not to argue that ProtoHedge universally dominates vanilla Deep Hedging on every metric and every market. Nor is our contribution to propose a new prototype architecture. Rather, our contribution is to test whether the central ProtoHedge idea --- interpretable prototype-based deep hedging --- remains viable and informative in an empirical setting where realistic market frictions, regime changes, and historical path structure matter.
+The original ProtoHedge study showed that prototype-based policies can remain
+competitive in Black--Scholes and stochastic-volatility simulations. Historical
+option data pose a materially different test: volatility and liquidity vary
+over time, option observations are noisy, trading is costly and constrained,
+and market paths contain regime shifts and discontinuities. The
+interpretability claim is also stronger on real data, because a useful
+prototype should correspond to a recognizable historical state rather than
+only to a simulator-generated configuration.
 
-The remainder of the paper operationalizes that question. Section~\ref{sec:experimental_framework} describes the real-data experimental design, including the construction of historical episodes, the liabilities, the model set, and the training protocol. Section~\ref{sec:empirical_results} then reports the real-data results, including panel-level performance, position-bound behavior, interpretability diagnostics, and the path-dependent Asian-option extension.
+Accordingly, this paper evaluates whether ProtoHedge remains competitive with
+matched black-box Deep Hedging and transparent delta-style baselines while
+avoiding excessive reliance on hard position bounds. It also tests whether
+prototype use is concentrated, regime-dependent, and retrievable as concrete
+historical episodes. We conduct this evaluation for both terminal European
+short calls and path-dependent Asian short calls, allowing the
+performance--interpretability tradeoff to be assessed under two economically
+distinct liabilities.
 
 
 
